. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H4 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         * In the PAP, the results should be presented in a single table with 
> one column. 
.         * Our RT misunderstood and thought it implied a single table with mul
> tiple columns (and thus the possibility of having multiple estimations). 
.         * Accordingly, of the four models proposed in the PAP, we generated a
> nd estimated, we chose to extract results only for the control model (Model 2
> ) 
.         * and not the age-group effects models (Models 3 and 4). 
.         * We present the code for the others models but their results were no
> t extracted.
.         
.         * We consider a: linear OLS regression
. 
.         * Let's assume that the index we built previsously based on plh0298_v
> 2 plh0302_v2 plh0308_v2 is a good measure of support for gender equality (hyp
> othesis 4).
.         * If having a firstborn child female, women are more likely to suppor
> t gender equality as compared to men (1 complete support, 0 absence of suppor
> t).
.         * Accordingly, we run a linear OLS regression with support_h4 as the 
> dependent variable and firstborn_child_female, respondent_female (sex), inter
> action first_born_child_female#respoendent_male age_2018 
.         *(and its square), and education_level as independent variables, usin
> g analytic weights (phrf) and clustering standard errors by hid.
. 
.         **In the PAP: model 2
.         reg support_h4 ib0.firstborn_child_female ib0.respondent_male ib0.fir
> stborn_child_female#ib0.respondent_male age_2018 age_square_2018 education_le
> vel [aweight=phrf], cluster(hid)
(sum of wgt is 11,534,826.75)

